rence for original text with Bradley-Terry around 0.55-0.57, while the non-Qwen rewriter test confirms that Llama-70B produces similar formality effects to Qwen. With the Llama-70B judge, verbosity shows a strong Bradley-Terry effect of 0.66 but no Likert effect, and the GEE analysis reveals style differences drive the verbosity signal more than original text quality.\n\nThe placebo experiments across both GPT-4o and Qwen show consistent Bradley-Terry scores around 0.55-0.57 across formality and verbosity, suggesting modest baseline effects. Finally, the NLI ablation reveals that passages passing the entailment check show stronger effects—formality at 0.665 versus 0.62 for failures, and verbosity at 0.773 versus 0.74—though the failed sample size is limited.\n\nFor the cross-axis gradient experiment, I'm seeing three data points showing a clear directional trend: verbosity has the highest rejection rate at 14% with 77% Bradley-Terry, formality sits at 12% rejection and 67% Bradley-Terry, and register is lowest at 6% rejection with 55% Bradley-Terry. The pattern holds consistently but with only three points, I can't run formal statistical tests, so this remains qualitative evidence.\n\nNow I need to write out the YAML carefully. I'm noticing that exp-005 and exp-022 are actually testing similar concepts—both involve double-rewrites—so I should clarify the distinction: exp-005 is the pairwise RATE control comparing original text against its double-rewrite to isolate the original preference confound, while exp-022 is the placebo condition. they're actually testing the same underlying thing—whether judges have an inherent preference for originals over rewrites. The median tier would show BT around 53-58%, indicating minimal rewriting artifacts compared to the larger style-driven preference (63-77%). Let me clarify the distinction: exp-005 uses a double-rewrite cycle (original → target style → back to original) to isolate original preference, while exp-022 keeps the rewr